\f1\fs22 The conference room felt too small for the weight of the previous night's revelations. Eloise sat at the head of the table. Sarah perched stiffly at Eloise\'92s right side, her pregnancy making itself known through an insistent backache as she reviewed the test data. Johnny lounged with his typical arrogance, while Chip clutched a steaming coffee cup like a security blanket. Eloise occupied her customary position at the far end, fingers steepled as she observed the proceedings with calculating eyes.\
"So what exactly did we achieve?" Sarah demanded, scanning the holographic displays. "Besides confirming that interdimensional beings have laundry problems?"\
"We've established stable multi-disc resonance and achieved our first verified interdimensional communication," Johnny began proudly. "The potential applications are limitless\'97"\
"A message about missing socks," Chip muttered into his coffee. "Hardly the cosmic wisdom we were expecting."\
"Communication is communication," Eloise said mildly. "The linguistic patterns suggest genuine cross-dimensional contact."\
\'91Multiple universes dealing with the same mundane concerns\'97it's actually quite profound when you think about it,\'92 Johnny added.\
"What concerns me more," Chip said carefully, "are the seismic readings. Two separate earthquake events during our testing period\'97that can't be coincidence."\
Sarah pulled up the geological data. "The local tremor, magnitude 3.8, I can understand might be related to our experiments. But this one\'97" She highlighted the remote reading. "Magnitude 4.2 in the central Gibson Desert? That's over a thousand kilometres away."\
\'93Twelve hundred and twenty-four kilometres, to be precise,\'94 Johnny said, showing off, then quickly added as his eyes flickered nervously. "Pure coincidence.\'94\
"Is it?" Sarah leaned forward with interest.\
"Perhaps our resonance effects propagate farther than anticipated. The Earth's crust responding to dimensional stress.\'94 Solo scratched his nose in a fashion so vigorous and socially nonchalant, only a man of advanced age would dare.\
"Now you're being ridiculous," Johnny scoffed at the Sage, offending everyone, curiously himself included."There's no mechanism for\'97"\
"Actually," Chip interrupted, "the timing correlation is... troubling. Both events occurred within minutes of our peak resonance phase."\
Johnny couldn\'92t let Chip have the last word. "I should mention the Gibson Desert site was actually\'85 within our predicted resonance propagation zone."\
The room fell silent. Eloise's expression remained neutral, but Sarah caught the sharp look she shot at Johnny.\
"Predicted resonance propagation zone,\'94 Sarah's mocked, voice was dangerously quiet. "You're telling me we expected to trigger earthquakes?"\
"Not intentionally!\'94 Johnny tried to cover, as the Sages\'97especially Chilo\'97became ever more nervously rigid in their gestures. \
"Well, the models did suggest potential tectonic responses to large-scale temporal distortions," Johnny continued, oblivious to the growing tension. "The Gibson Desert coordinates were actually one of our primary calculation targets\'97remote location, stable geology, minimal infrastructure risk. Perfect for testing seismic propagation effects."\
\'93Mr Stoat,\'94 Eloise said quietly, a warning note in her voice.\
But Johnny was too caught up in his intellectual superiority to notice. "The precision is quite remarkable, really. We managed to generate a controlled seismic event at exactly the predicted coordinates and magnitude range. It demonstrates unprecedented accuracy in\'97"\
"In what?" Sarah's eyes narrowed dangerously. "In triggering earthquakes on command?"\
"Sarah," Chip said carefully, "I've seen enough Fridge Force files to recognize weapons testing protocols when I see them."\
"Now you're both being dramatic," Eloise said smoothly. "All advanced research has unexpected side effects. The important thing is that we're making genuine scientific breakthroughs."\
"Are we?" Sarah stood abruptly, ignoring the protest from her lower back. "Or are we developing the ability to destabilize geological formations anywhere on the planet?"\
"The interdimensional communication alone represents a paradigm shift," Eloise deflected. "Think of the implications for human knowledge\'97"\
"I'm thinking of the implications for human warfare," Sarah shot back. "Remote earthquake generation? Targeted seismic weapons?"\
"All technology has dual-use potential," Johnny said defensively. "Communications, medicine, defense\'97"\
"Defense?" Sarah's laugh was hollow. "What's defensive about triggering earthquakes a thousand kilometers away?"\
\'91Twelve hundred and twenty\'97\'92\
\'91Shut up, Johnny.\'92 Sarah was losing her temper and Little Clinton kicked.\
Chilo observed quietly, "Perhaps we should consider the broader strategic applications of such precision."\
"Strategic applications," Sarah repeated flatly.\
Eloise said carefully, \'93We shouldn't limit our thinking about what we've achieved. The ability to influence geological stability from a distance... well, it raises interesting possibilities."\
Sarah gathered her tablets, movements sharp with anger. "This meeting is adjourned. I need time to review exactly what kind of research we're conducting here."\
"Sarah, wait\'97" Johnny attempted.\
"No." She cut him off. "I've waited quite enough. While I've been marvelling at interdimensional laundry concerns, I've apparently been perfecting earthquake weapons."\
